aiao | Department of Civil and Natural Resources Engineering hosts many active research groups and has an international reputation.\nOur research groups include:\nFire Engineering\nGeotechnical Engineering\nFluid Mechanics\nHydrological and Ecological Engineering\nEnvironmental Engineering\nTimber Engineering\nBridge Engineering\nEarthquake Engineering\nSteel and Concrete Engineering\nTransportation Engineering\nConstruction Management\nRenewable Energy.\nCourses\nkeyboard_arrow_down\nCivil Engineering\nSee all postgraduate courses in Civil Engineering\nCareer opportunities\nkeyboard_arrow_down\nCivil Engineering\nPostgraduate study can bring many career benefits eg, specialist skills and enhanced knowledge, entry into specific occupations, higher starting salary/progression rates, research capability/achievement, and evidence of high academic attainment/self-discipline.\nFind out more about what you can do with a degree in Civil Engineering.\nBrochures\nPostgraduate Prospectus 2027\nWhakatairanga T\u0101ura\nDownload\n(application/pdf,\n7 MB)\ndownload\nDownload\nInternational Prospectus 2027\nDownload\n(application/pdf,\n8 MB)\ndownload\nDownload\nUndergraduate subjects\nCivil Engineering\nQualifications\nPostgraduate Certificate in Civil Engineering\nPostgraduate Certificate in Engineering\nMaster of Civil Engineering\nMaster of Engineering\nMaster of Engineering Studies\nDoctor of Philosophy\nContact us\nScholarships\nScholarships\nRelated subjects\nChoose an area that you are interested in and learn how UC\u2019s range of study options can let you study what you want to.\nEngineering\n12 October 2026\nLearn how to use physical science and mathematics to solve complex problems.\nEngineering\nLearn how to use physical science and mathematics to solve complex problems.\nEngineering\nPressing this button will open your email client.\nPressing this button will start a call.\nEngineering\nLearn how to use physical science and mathematics to solve complex problems.\nForest Engineering\n23 April 2026\nConstruct and evaluate the operational systems of the forest industry.\nForest Engineering\nConstruct and evaluate the operational systems of the forest industry.\nForest Engineering\nPressing this button will open your email client.\nPressing this button will start a call.\nForest Engineering\nConstruct and evaluate the operational systems of the forest industry.\nclose\nClose\nPrivacy Preferences\nBy clicking \"Accept All Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to enhance site navigation, analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts.\nAccept all\nDecline all\nCookies policy\nBack to top\nexpand_less\nFind it\nContact us\nAcademic schools and departments\nCareers for students\nMaps\nPolicies\nService departments\nIT Services and Software\nCommunities\nDo it\nAdmission and Enrolment\nLibrary\nMedia enquiries\nPartnering with UC\nRaise a concern\nSupport and wellbeing\nWays to give\nWork at UC\nStudy it\nQualifications\nSubjects\nArts\nBusiness\nEducation and Teaching\nEngineering\nHealth\nLaw and Criminal Justice\nScience\nTools\nAKO | LEARN\nmyUC\nLibrary\nIT self-service portal\nStudent email and Office 365\nTimetable planner\nStaff intranet\nLatest\nNews\nEvents\n150 years\nGeneral enquiries\n0800 827 748 (within NZ)\n+64 3 369 3999\ninfo@canterbury.ac.nz\nInternational enquiries\n+64 3 288 0702\nAsk a question\nMore contact details\nSee all contacts\nEmergency contact details\nExt: 92111 (from a campus landline)\nDirect dial: 0800 823 637\nEmergency information\nCopyright\nPrivacy\nCookies declaration\nPayment data\nUniversity of Canterbury", "links": [["Skip to main content", "https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/study/academic-study/subjects/civil-engineering-pg#maincontent"], ["", "https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/"], ["Future students", "https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/communities/future-students"], ["Current students", "https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/communities/current-students"], ["International", "https:/